course: everyday
lesson: 424
title: Teamwork, strategy and Minecraft building
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Kai, Priya and Leo decided to build a whole city in Minecraft. On day one all three built a castle, in the same spot, on top of each other, and it looked like a sandwich made by a tornado. On day two they stopped and made a plan: Priya does the roads, Leo the houses, Kai the giant lighthouse nobody asked for. They argued about the lighthouse for twenty minutes. In the end it stayed, and on the last day the whole street of houses was lit by it at night. «Fine,» said Priya, «the lighthouse was a good idea.» Kai has had it printed on a T-shirt in his head ever since.
explain: Theo plans team games with drawings: arrows, circles and a stick figure of himself with a cape. His team laughed at the cape and then won three matches in a row, because everyone knew where to go. Noah's team got a brand-new player who kept walking into walls. Instead of kicking her out, they showed her the controls for ten minutes. Two weeks later she was the one rescuing them. Leo and Kai spent a whole weekend stuck on one puzzle game level; Kai saw the pattern, Leo saw the trick, and neither could have done it alone.
explain: Games taught some unexpected things. Jayden learned «inventory», «durability» (how long a tool lasts before it wears out) and «respawn» (coming back to life in a game) from a game, and then used «durable» correctly in a science lesson, which shocked his teacher so much she wrote it on the board. Zara built her school project, a Roman villa, in Minecraft and showed it on the class screen. Priya built a model of her own house for her mum's birthday, including the cat's bed, the leaky tap and her brother's messy room, done with brown blocks.
explain: Ruby teaches her grandma Minecraft over a video call every Thursday. Grandma dug straight down on day one and fell into lava, then shouted «I'm melting!» for the whole street to hear. Now she has a little cottage with sheep and has named every sheep after a relative. Ollie joined an online games club run by his school, where a teacher keeps watch, and made a new friend from another class who also loves building trains. Mia organised a building competition with three themes and a fair rule: nobody judges their own build.
fact: Team games and shared builds go better when the team makes a plan and shares out the jobs.
fact: In a good team, people's different strengths fit together, so the team can do what nobody could do alone.
fact: Explaining a plan with a drawing helps everyone see where to go and what to do.
fact: Helping a new player learn makes the whole team stronger in the end.
fact: Games can teach new words, planning, problem solving and patience.
fact: Building games like Minecraft can be used for school projects and models of real places.
fact: Playing with family over a video call is a way to stay close to people who live far away.
fact: Online clubs run by a school, with a teacher watching, are a safe way to meet new friends who like the same games.
fact: A fair competition has clear rules, and nobody judges their own work.
fact: Agreeing on a plan often takes some arguing, and that is normal if everyone listens.
exercise: Why did the first day of the city build go wrong? | All three built in the same spot with no plan | The game crashed and lost all their work from that day | Priya refused to build anything at all | Leo built only underground tunnels
exercise: What did they do on day two that worked? | Made a plan and shared out the jobs | Each started their own separate world | Let Kai decide everything all by himself | Copied a whole city from a video online
exercise: How did Priya feel about the lighthouse in the end? | She admitted it was a good idea | She was still angry about it | She secretly knocked it down | She never noticed it was there
exercise: Why did Theo's team win three matches in a row? | Everyone knew the plan and where to go | Theo's cape gave the whole team special powers | The other teams were not trying | They played on the easiest level
exercise: What did Noah's team do with the new player? | Showed her the controls patiently | Kicked her out of the team at once | Told her to go and watch videos alone | Let her keep walking into walls all match
exercise: What happened two weeks later with the new player? | She was the one rescuing the team | She had left the game for good | She was still walking into walls | She started her own rival team
exercise: Why did Leo and Kai solve the puzzle together? | Each saw a different part of the answer | Kai finally looked up the answer online | Leo pressed every button until it worked | The game gave them a free hint
exercise: What did Jayden learn from a game? | New words like «durable» and «respawn» | How to stay up all night playing | How to talk to players in other countries | How to build a real wooden house
exercise: How did Zara use Minecraft for school? | She built a Roman villa for her project | She played it during a maths lesson | She taught the class how to fight zombies | She used it to copy a friend's homework
exercise: What did Priya build for her mum's birthday? | A model of their own house | A castle with a tall lighthouse | A Roman villa for her school project | A farm full of sheep with names
exercise: What happened to Grandma on her first day in Minecraft? | She dug straight down and fell into lava | She built a whole castle in five minutes flat | She beat Ruby in a race | She could not find the start button
exercise: Why are the Thursday calls good for Ruby and Grandma? | They stay close even though they live apart | Grandma wants to become a famous gamer for money | Ruby gets out of homework on Thursdays | The calls are free only on Thursdays
exercise: Why is Ollie's school games club a safe way to make friends? | A teacher runs it and keeps watch | Everyone in it is exactly the same age | Nobody is allowed to talk to anyone in it | It only meets once a year, in the summer
exercise: What fair rule did Mia make for her competition? | Nobody judges their own build | The oldest player always wins | Every build must be a lighthouse | Only Mia can vote for the winner
exercise: Is arguing about a plan always a sign of a bad team? | No, it is normal if everyone listens | Yes, good teams never disagree | Yes, the team must split up at once | No, the loudest person should just win
test: «we all built a castle in the same spot lol it's a mess». What should they do now? | Stop, plan, and share out the jobs | Keep building faster on top of it | Delete the world and quit the game | Let whoever shouts loudest decide
test: Noah's mate: «the new girl is useless, kick her». What's the wiser move? | Show her the controls for a few minutes | Kick her out before she ruins the match for everyone | Tell her to go and play somewhere else | Ignore her and hope she leaves
test: Theo's drawing has arrows and a stick man with a cape. What's it for? | Showing the team the plan clearly | Making the other team laugh at him | Practising his art instead of playing | Proving that he is the boss of the team
test: Teacher, amazed: «Jayden, where did you learn durable?» What's the likely answer? | From a game, where things wear out | From reading a dictionary every night | From his grandma's knitting club | From a video about building bridges
test: Gran on the call: «I'm MELTING!!» What has just happened in the game? | She fell into lava in Minecraft | Her heating has broken at home | She is baking a cake too hot | Her tablet is getting too warm
test: Leo stuck on the puzzle all Saturday, Kai stuck too. Together on Sunday they do it. Why? | Their different ideas fit together | It got easier because it was Sunday | The game felt sorry for them | They both guessed at random
test: Mia's building contest: «can I judge my own one?» What should Mia say? | No, nobody judges their own build | Yes, if you promise to be fair | Yes, but only give yourself half marks | No, only Mia is allowed to judge
test: Ollie wants new friends who like trains. Which is safest? | The school's games club with a teacher | A random chat room full of strangers | Posting his address in a train game | Adding everyone who sends a request
test: Zara: «can a game really help with homework??» What's true? | Yes, she built a Roman villa for her project | No, games and homework never mix, not even a little bit | Only if the game is about maths | Only on weekends and holidays
test: «the lighthouse is pointless, Kai». Twenty minutes of arguing. Then? | They listened, kept it, and it lit the street | Priya quit the world and never played with them again | Kai knocked down Priya's roads | They asked a stranger to decide it
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a collaborative construction is more successful following the preparation of a plan. | The allocation of tasks prevents duplication and conflict | The game rewards plans with additional materials | Planning renders the construction automatically complete | The plan removes the necessity for any communication
exam: It is given that a team admits an inexperienced player and instructs her patiently. Select the outcome most consistent with the account. | The player subsequently contributes to the team's success | The player is permanently unable to improve | The team is penalised for assisting her | The match is cancelled by the game's operators
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an online games club administered by a school? | It is supervised by a teacher and therefore safer | It is open to any stranger without supervision | It requires members to disclose their addresses | It is prohibited for pupils of primary age
exam: Determine which of the following constitutes a fair rule for a building competition. | That no participant shall judge his or her own build | That the organiser shall be the sole judge | That the eldest participant shall be declared winner | That each participant shall award himself full marks
